WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:05.000
Acceleration support determines whether local AI feels interactive or merely possible.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.120
NVIDIA hardware commonly uses CUDA,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.260
a mature compute platform supported by many AI runtimes.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.135
AMD workflows may use ROCm where the operating system and GPU are supported,

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:18.678
while Vulkan backends can provide broader cross-vendor acceleration for compatible tools.

00:00:18.678 --> 00:00:22.340
Support varies by model, driver, runtime build, and operating system,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.480
so a GPU brand alone does not guarantee a working path.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.185
Think of the stack as a chain.

00:00:27.185 --> 00:00:30.075
The operating system recognizes the GPU through its driver.

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:33.450
The runtime must be compiled for a compatible acceleration backend.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.540
That backend must support the GPU architecture and the numerical formats used by the

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:38.050
model.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.760
If any link is incompatible, the application may fail to load, silently run on the CPU,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.360
or offload only part of the workload.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.600
Confirm acceleration through runtime logs and GPU utilization rather than assuming that

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.245
installation success means the GPU is active.

00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:55.445
GPU offload places model layers or operations on the graphics processor while retaining

00:00:55.445 --> 00:00:57.760
the remainder in system memory and on the CPU.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.560
Full offload is usually faster when the model fits in VRAM.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.935
Partial offload can make a larger model usable,

00:01:02.935 --> 00:01:05.761
but data movement and CPU computation reduce speed.

00:01:05.761 --> 00:01:09.250
CPU-only inference remains a valuable fallback for troubleshooting, embeddings,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.400
and smaller models, though interactive generation is typically slower.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.110
Version discipline matters.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.175
Record the known-good GPU driver, acceleration runtime, and application build.

00:01:18.175 --> 00:01:19.920
Do not update every layer at once.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.155
When a change is necessary,

00:01:21.155 --> 00:01:25.020
verify one representative model before modifying the rest of the environment.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.990
A slightly older stable stack that consistently uses acceleration is more useful than a

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.160
newer combination whose compatibility has not been proven.
